## The handler
{{ if $py }}
- `app/handler.py` defines `handler(event, context)`, which takes an HTTP
  API event (payload format 2.0, a dict) and returns a dict: a status code,
  headers and a string body. [review]
- Create boto3 clients and resources and read configuration at module scope,
  once per execution environment; warm invocations reuse them and their
  connections. Never create one inside the handler. [review]
- Read `os.environ` only in `app/handler.py`, once, and fail at start-up
  with a message naming the variable when one is missing. [review]
- Keep the handler to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they
  need (a store, a log), so tests call them without Lambda. [review]
- Never `sys.exit()` or `os._exit()`: they end the execution environment
  mid-invocation. Raise, or return an error response. [review]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: the next invocation in
  the same environment would see it. [review]
{{- else }}
- `src/handler.ts` exports `handler`, which takes an `APIGatewayProxyEventV2`
  and returns a result: a status code, headers and a string body. API
  Gateway's HTTP API sends payload format 2.0. [review]
- Create SDK clients and read configuration at module scope, once per
  execution environment; warm invocations reuse them and their connections.
  Never create a client inside the handler. [review]
- Read `process.env` only in `src/handler.ts`, once, and fail at start-up
  with a message naming the variable when one is missing. [review]
- Keep the handler to wiring; the work is in functions that take what they
  need (a store, a log), so tests call them without Lambda. [review]
- Await every promise before returning: work left running after the handler
  returns is frozen with the environment and may never finish.
  [lint: @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises]
- Never `process.exit()`: it kills the execution environment mid-invocation.
  [lint: no-restricted-properties]
- Don't keep request data in module-scope variables: the next invocation in
  the same environment would see it. [review]
{{- end }}

## Requests and responses

{{ if $py -}}
- Validate every input at the boundary: path parameters, query strings,
  headers and the body, narrowed from `object` with `isinstance` checks. API
{{- else -}}
- Validate every input at the boundary: path parameters, query strings,
  headers and the body, narrowed from `unknown` with runtime checks. API
{{- end }}
  Gateway delivers whatever the client sent. [review]
- A body can arrive base64-encoded (`isBase64Encoded`); decode it before
  parsing. [review]
- Answer expected failures with their status: 400 for bad input, 403, 404,
  409 for a conflict, 413 for too large, and 500 only for a bug or an
  outage. The body is JSON with a `message` a client can act on. [review]
- Never return a stack trace, an SDK error's text or an internal name to a
  client; log it instead, with the request id. [review]
- Set `content-type` on every response. [review]

## Logs

{{ if $py -}}
- Log structured JSON, one entry per line, through the `Log` the code is
  given; never `print`. [lint: T201]
{{ else -}}
- Log structured JSON, one entry per line, through the `Log` the code is
  given; never `console`. [lint: no-console]
{{ end -}}
- Each entry has a `level`, a `message` and the request id; errors say what
  failed and with which input, never a secret, a token or user data. [review]
- The function's log group is created by the stack, with a retention.
  [nag: none; test: log groups keep logs for a set time]
- The function logs in JSON format (`loggingFormat: LoggingFormat.JSON`), so
  Lambda's own entries are structured too. [review]

## DynamoDB

- Model the table for its access patterns: every read on a request path is
  a `GetItem` or a `Query` on a key or an index, never a `Scan`. [review]
{{- if $py }}
- Use boto3's `Table` resource so items are plain dicts; numbers come back
  as `Decimal`. Validate what a read returns before using it, since another
  writer may have stored something else. [review]
{{- else }}
- Use the document client (`@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`) so items are plain
  objects; validate what a read returns before using it, since another
  writer may have stored something else. [review]
{{- end }}
- Writes are idempotent: a `PutItem` of the whole item, or a conditional
  write (`ConditionExpression`) that refuses a second apply. Never a blind
  `ADD` on a retried request. [review]
- Tables are on demand (`PAY_PER_REQUEST`) unless the issue states a steady
  load. [review]
- Point-in-time recovery on. [nag: AwsSolutions-DDB3]
- Encrypted at rest with a KMS key (`TableEncryption.AWS_MANAGED` or
  customer-managed). [test: encrypts every table]
- The table outlives the stack: `RemovalPolicy.RETAIN` and deletion
  protection. [test: says what happens to stateful resources; review]

{{ if $py -}}

## boto3

- boto3 is a `dependency` pinned exactly in `pyproject.toml`, and the stack
  bundles the version `uv.lock` pins into the function, not the runtime's
  copy. Its type stubs (`types-boto3[<service>]`) are dev dependencies at
  the same version; add the extra for each service you call. [review]
- A client's region and credentials come from the Lambda environment; never
  pass keys or set a region in code. [review]
- Wrap each service in one module behind a `Protocol` the rest of the code
  depends on, typed with the stubs' `TypedDict`s under `TYPE_CHECKING`;
  tests pass a fake. [review]
- boto3 retries throttling and transient errors itself; don't add a retry
  loop around it. Catch what you can answer (a failed condition,
  `ConditionalCheckFailedException`, is a 409), let the rest reach the
  handler's 500. [review]
{{ else -}}

## The AWS SDK v3

- Import only the clients you use (`@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb`), never a
  whole SDK; esbuild bundles them into the function. [review]
- Pin every `@aws-sdk/*` package to the same exact version. [review]
- A client's region and credentials come from the Lambda environment; never
  pass keys or set a region in code. [review]
- Wrap each service in one module behind an interface the rest of the code
  depends on; tests fake that interface. [review]
- The SDK retries throttling and transient errors itself; don't add a retry
  loop around it. Catch what you can answer (a failed condition is a 409),
  let the rest reach the handler's 500. [review]
{{ end }}
## API Gateway HTTP API

- Every route is authorized: IAM (`HttpIamAuthorizer`), a JWT authorizer or a
  Lambda authorizer. [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG4]
- The stage writes access logs to a log group with a retention.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-APIG1; test]
- The stage is throttled (`throttle: { rateLimit, burstLimit }`), so a burst
  can't run up the bill or starve other functions. [review]
- Routes name their method and path (`GET /items/{id}`); no `ANY /{proxy+}`
  that hands routing to the function. [review]

## Lambda configuration in the stack

{{ if $py -}}
- A `Function` whose code is `Code.fromAsset` with local bundling: uv
  installs the packages `uv.lock` pins, without the dev group, as wheels for
  arm64 Linux, next to `app/`. Never Docker, which CI and agents lack: the
  local bundler returns true or throws. [review]
- The newest Python runtime. [nag: AwsSolutions-L1]
{{ else -}}
- `NodejsFunction`, bundled by the installed esbuild
  (`forceDockerBundling: false`); never Docker, which CI and agents lack.
  [review]
- The newest Node.js runtime. [nag: AwsSolutions-L1]
{{ end -}}
- `arm64` (Graviton): cheaper per millisecond and lower energy. [review]
- Memory and timeout set for the work, the timeout under API Gateway's 30
  seconds. [review]
- Its own role, granted only what the code calls; no AWS managed policies.
  [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM4]
- No wildcard permissions: grants (`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`),
  never a hand-written `*`. [nag: AwsSolutions-IAM5; test: no wildcard action]
- No secrets in environment variables: they are visible in the console and
  the template. Read them from Secrets Manager at start-up. [review]

## The CDK app

- `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong to the Standard; the
  Framework's stacks are in `infra/stacks.ts`, which `defineStacks` returns.
  [review]
- Stacks are environment-agnostic: no `env` from `CDK_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT`, no
  `fromLookup`, so synth needs no account. [review]
- Every stack carries the Standard's tags (`c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system`, `c4factory:container`). [test: tags every stack]
- Every stateful resource says its removal policy. [test]
- A CDK Nag finding is fixed in the resource. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`), on the construct
  the finding's path names or an ancestor of it, names one finding
  on one construct, with a reason specific to this system. [review]
- `cdk.json`'s feature flags are the ones `cdk init` recommends for the
  pinned CDK; a task never turns one off. [review]

## Tests

- No test calls AWS, opens a socket or needs credentials. [review]
- Stacks are tested with `Template.fromStack` from `aws-cdk-lib/assertions`:
  `hasResourceProperties`, `resourceCountIs`, `Match`. Assert what the
  issue needs (a route, a grant, a setting), not the whole template. [review]
{{- if $py }}
- Code that calls AWS is tested with pytest through its `Protocol`, with a
  fake that records calls and answers from memory; never `moto` or a
  patched boto3. [review]
- Handlers are tested by building an HTTP API event dict and checking the
  status code and body. [review]
{{- else }}
- Code that calls AWS is tested through its interface with a fake that
  records commands and answers from memory. [review]
- Handlers are tested by building an `APIGatewayProxyEventV2` and checking
  the status code and body. [review]
{{- end }}
